# In a replay `why total` names the write that gave `total` its value at
# this stop: the third pass of line 21 holds 2, which line 21 wrote on the
# loop's second pass (the first pass added 0, which changed nothing), in
# run() on the main task, replacing 0; the write's stack follows. `why`
# runs the replay again behind the stop and comes back to it, so `c` runs
# the program on to its end from there. The helper task's tally races the
# loop, so only the main task's total is asserted.
# The fixture must stay in jet fmt form; line 21 adds `d` to `total`.
fixture: ReplayReverse.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
step: jet run --record=why prog.jet
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "total 6 tally " # the program's own output among the session's
step: jet debug --replay=why prog.jet
stdin: replay_why_names_the_last_write.stdin
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "(Jet) why total\ntotal = 2, set at prog.jet:21 in run() (task 1)\n  before: 0\n  #0  run()  at prog.jet:21\n" # the last write, its task, the value it replaced and its stack
stdout: ~ "(Jet) why\nWhy needs a local or a field path, as `why total`\n" # the command and its answer among the session's output
stdout: ~ "(Jet) why nothing\n`nothing` is not a local" # a name the frame lacks is refused
stdout: ~ "(Jet) c\ntotal 6 tally " # the session is back at its stop and runs on to the end
stdout: ~ "Program finished\n" # the session runs the program to its end
stdout+stderr: !~ "replay diverged" # the replay stays faithful to the recording
stdout+stderr: !~ "didn't come back" # the session comes back to its stop
